response:
 If $s$ feet is the distance an athlete has run after $n$ turns, so $s\equiv0\pmod{50.5}$, then the number of turns is determined by $n=\left\lceil\frac{s}{50.5}\right\rceil-1$. In this case, $s=5280$, $r=\left\lceil\frac{s}{50.5}\right\rceil-1=\left\lceil\frac{5280}{505}\right\rceil-1=11-1=10$, and $\alpha=\frac{2\pi}{4}\cdot r=\frac\pi2\cdot10=\frac{5\pi}2$, so after $s$ feet Joneal stops when he is at angle $\alpha$ (where $0\le\alpha<\frac{5\pi}{2}$). $\alpha$ endpoints the fourth quadrant $\left[0,\frac{3\pi}2\right)$. In general, angles in quadrant  $A$ are in the interval $\left(-\frac{3\pi}{4},-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)$, $B$ in $\left[\frac{3\pi}{4},\pi\right)$, $C$ in $\left[-\pi,-\frac{3\pi}{4}\}\right.$, $D$ in $\left\{\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{3\pi}{4}\right]\right$. ($\alpha=\frac{5\pi}{2}$ is equivalent to an angle of $\frac{\pi}{2}$). Thus, Joneal stops when he is at the negative $y$-axis in quadrant $B$ (clockwise from positive $x$-axis) to the start point $S$ on negative $x$-axis.
